      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner is lost, so begin with the key idea and ask one small conceptual question without solving the intervals.",
      "content": "Let’s take it one step at a time. On a momentum–time graph, is the net force shown by the graph’s height or its slope?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I’m not sure. Maybe the height?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has confused momentum values with force. Clarify that force is the slope, then scaffold one interval with a single arithmetic question.",
      "content": "Good try! Net force is the slope: F_net = Δp/Δt. For 3–4 s, momentum changes from −10 to 20. What is the magnitude of that change, |20 − (−10)|?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "30? The negatives make me unsure."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly found the momentum-change magnitude. Verify the slope magnitudes for all four equal one-second intervals, then identify the greatest. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"abs((20-0)/(1-0)), abs((10-20)/(2-1)), abs((-10-10)/(3-2)), abs((20-(-10))/(4-3))\") -> (20, 10, 20, 30)] Step-by-step arithmetic: |20−0|/1 = 20 N; |10−20|/1 = 10 N; |−10−10|/1 = 20 N; |20−(−10)|/1 = 30 N. The greatest magnitude is for 3–4 s.",
      "content": "Exactly—the change is 30. The force magnitudes for A–D are 20 N, 10 N, 20 N, and 30 N. So the greatest is during 3–4 s: **D**."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "D",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nchanges = [(20-0, 1), (10-20, 1), (-10-10, 1), (20-(-10), 1)]\nmagnitudes = [abs(sp.Rational(dp, dt)) for dp, dt in changes]\nprint([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"][magnitudes.index(max(magnitudes))])",
    "techniques": [
      "Interpret the gradient of a momentum–time graph as net force",
      "Calculate the magnitude of the slope for each interval",
      "Compare force magnitudes and select the greatest"
    ]
  }
}

